  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nSynoptic Overview:\n\nUpper-air analysis indicates the region is sitting under a\nquasi-zonal flow pattern aloft with a slight northwesterly flow\ncomponent. The polar jet extends through the Ohio Valley,\nleaving diffuse high pressure at the surface with clear skies. \n\nDry and Mild through Tuesday:\n\nThe above pattern will persist through Tuesday, allowing for the\ncontinuation of dry weather and seasonable (if not a bit above-\naverage) afternoon temperatures. Look for highs in the mid 60s\nand overnight lows in the low 40s to upper 30s areawide.\n\nPrecipitation Chances Wednesday through Friday:\n\nEnsembles depict general agreement in digging a series of \nphasing shortwaves through the eastern CONUS on Wednesday.\nCluster analysis suggests the greatest divergence among ensemble\nmembers is in the timing and speed of these impulses. The \nassociated surface low is progged to deepen as it transits the \nOhio Valley, dragging a well defined cold front to its south \nalong with it. The southerly return flow ahead of this front \nwill be brief and limited, resulting in only modest moisture \nadvection into the region. Supportive upper- level dynamics and \nample lift along the front provide high confidence in rain \nchances (70-90%) across the forecast area, but the forecasted quick\npace of the front and lack of deep moisture will limit total \naccumulations. For reference, NBM probabilities of receiving at \nleast 0.10 inches of QPF are between 50-75% across the area but \ndrop to less than 25% for half an inch. Rain chances increase \nfrom west to east beginning Wednesday afternoon, are highest \nWednesday evening, and decrease Thursday morning. It's possible \nsome very light precipitation persists across eastern Kentucky\nthrough the day Thursday in the vicinity of the low, but \nadditional accumulations appear minimal. \n\nFurthermore, if enough moisture can stick around into Thursday \nnight or Friday morning, temperatures right around the freezing \nmark could support a few non-accumulating flurries in this area.\nWith northerly winds and strong cold air advection on the back \nside of the low, this occurrence is not uncommon. A lot of \npieces have to come together for this scenario to actually \nmanifest, though, so confidence remains limited at this time. \n\nMuch Colder Thursday into the Weekend:\n\nAs previously mentioned, a much colder airmass will take hold \nbehind the rain chances on Thursday as the polar jet sinks \nsouthward over the eastern CONUS. Latest NBM percentile data\ndepict a clear dropoff in both high and low temperatures from\nWednesday to Thursday with small (3-4 degrees) interquartile\nspreads. That is, there is high confidence in temperatures 10-15\ndegrees colder over a 24-hr period. Friday appears to be the\ncoldest day of the forecast period, with highs in the 40s and\novernight minimums in the upper 20s to low 30s. Ensemble\nprobabilities of temperatures at or below the freezing mark\nrange between 75-95% areawide Friday night into Saturday\nmorning.",
